source: https://www.facebook.com/reel/1449664100263555
yield: as stated by the source, no weight given — see the reference component for a workable basis
ingredients:
  - item: pork shoulder
    note: no cut, shape or weight given by the source
  - item: soy sauce
  - item: hoisin sauce
  - item: five spice powder
  - item: other seasonings
    note: unspecified by the source beyond "other seasonings" — see the reference marinade below for a filled-in guess
steps:
  - combine soy sauce, hoisin sauce, five spice powder and the other seasonings into a marinade
  - add the pork shoulder, marinate a few hours up to overnight for best results
  - air fry, flipping and basting twice, until cooked through with a slight char
  - serve with rice and yu choy (or another Chinese green) — "you chou" in the auto-captions, almost certainly yu choy/choy sum
components:
  - name: reference marinade (not from the source — a typical char siu ratio, filled in so this is actually cookable; tune to taste)
    ingredients:
      - item: pork shoulder
        mass: 500
        basis: true
        note: cut into strips roughly 4-5cm wide and ~2cm thick — the timing below assumes this
      - item: light soy sauce
        mass: 30
        qty: 2
        unit: tbsp
      - item: hoisin sauce
        mass: 45
        qty: 3
        unit: tbsp
      - item: shaoxing wine
        mass: 15
        qty: 1
        unit: tbsp
        optional: true
      - item: five spice powder
        mass: 2
        qty: 0.5
        unit: tsp
      - item: brown sugar
        mass: 30
        qty: 2
        unit: tbsp
        note: or maltose, more traditional and stickier
      - item: garlic
        qty: 2
        unit: clove
        note: minced
      - item: sesame oil
        note: a few drops
      - item: red fermented bean curd (nam yu)
        optional: true
        note: traditional source of the classic red colour — a drop of red food colouring is the common shortcut, both purely cosmetic
    steps:
      - whisk everything but the pork together into a marinade
      - reserve a couple of tablespoons of the marinade, untouched by raw meat, for basting later — or plan to boil the used portion briefly before it touches the pork again
      - add the pork, turn to coat, marinate 4 hrs to overnight in the fridge, turning occasionally
  - name: procedure (air fryer)
    steps:
      - air fryer to 190°C
      - cook ~20 mins total, flipping and basting with the reserved marinade at roughly the 7 and 14 min marks — matches the source's "flip and baste twice"
      - watch the last few minutes closely — the sugar in the marinade catches fast in an air fryer's concentrated heat, so drop the temperature if it's colouring faster than it's cooking through
      - rest a few mins, slice, serve with rice and steamed yu choy or choy sum
  - name: procedure (oven, wire rack)
    steps:
      - oven to 200-210°C fan-forced — hotter than the air fryer figure above, see notes for why
      - wire rack over a foil-lined tray, strips spread with gaps between them for airflow underneath as well as over
      - bake ~25-30 mins for strips around 2cm thick, flipping and basting with the reserved marinade at roughly a third and two-thirds of the way through rather than fixed clock minutes — oven runtimes vary more than an air fryer's
      - go by colour and a probe (aim for the low-to-mid 60s°C core) rather than the clock, same as the air fryer version — thickness drives the time far more than weight does
      - if it's cooked through but hasn't caught the char the source describes, finish under a hot grill/broiler for 1-2 mins a side with a final baste — this is what recovers the concentrated blast an air fryer gives for free
      - rest a few mins, slice, serve with rice and steamed yu choy or choy sum
notes: |
  Source is a 30-second reel with no written recipe attached — the caption is a
  "comment 'recipe' and I'll send you the link" hook, so the actual quantities live behind
  that DM flow, not in the post. Everything under "reference marinade & timing" above is
  filled in from a standard char siu ratio, not from this source — treat it as a solid
  starting point rather than gospel, and adjust once you've made it once.

  The oven procedure exists because an air fryer isn't just a small oven at the same
  temperature — the fan sits much closer to the food in a much smaller chamber, so the air
  moving across the surface is far more concentrated. A full-size fan oven at the air
  fryer's dial temperature will cook slower and brown less in the same time, which is why
  the oven version runs 10-20°C hotter and allows a bit more time. The wire rack over a tray
  is the direct equivalent of the air fryer's perforated basket — air underneath as well as
  over, and the tray catches the sugary drips before they burn onto the oven floor.

  Because a big oven is gentler and more even rather than concentrated, it may finish the
  meat through without ever quite catching the char an air fryer gives almost by default —
  that's what the grill/broiler finish is for, and it's worth planning for rather than
  treating as a failure if the first attempt comes out pale.
